    <h1>Why Can AI Be So Confident and Still Be Wrong?</h1>

    <p class="article-info">Published October 21, 2026 · 7-minute read</p>

    <article class="article-body">
      <h2>1. How Does AI Generate Answers So Quickly?</h2>
      <p>At first, many of us wondered how artificial intelligence (AI) could answer our questions so quickly. It almost felt like magic. But how does AI actually generate answers?</p>
      <p>The answer is more complicated than it might seem.</p>
      <p>AI tools use models trained on large amounts of data, which can include text, code, images, and other forms of information. Large language models learn patterns and relationships in this data. When a user asks a question, the model uses what it has learned, together with the conversation's context, to generate a response.</p>
      <p>Some AI systems can also use external tools, such as web searches or calculators, to retrieve information or perform specific tasks. However, not every AI system has these capabilities, and not every question triggers their use.</p>
      <p>Although this process can produce remarkably useful and accurate answers, generating a plausible response is not the same as verifying that the response is true. Understanding this distinction is essential to understanding why AI sometimes makes mistakes.</p>

      <h2>2. Do All AI Systems Work in the Same Way?</h2>
      <p>Different AI systems can produce different answers to the same question. This is because they may use different model architectures, training data, training methods, and answer-generation settings. Some systems can also access external search tools or other sources of information.</p>
      <p>For example, asking ChatGPT, Gemini, and Claude the same question may produce different explanations or conclusions. These differences do not automatically mean that one model is more accurate than the other. Each answer must be evaluated based on its evidence and reasoning.</p>
      <p>A model's response may also be affected by the limitations of its training data, the information provided in the prompt, and whether it can access up-to-date sources. Consequently, even a highly capable AI system can generate information that is outdated, misleading, or incorrect.</p>

      <h2>3. Why Can AI Make Mistakes So Confidently?</h2>
      <p>One important problem with AI-generated answers is that they can sound convincing even when they contain errors. This phenomenon is often associated with AI hallucinations, a term used when a system generates content that is false, fabricated, or unsupported by available evidence.</p>
      <p>Language models generate responses based on patterns learned during training and the context in which they are used. Their ability to produce fluent language does not guarantee that every statement is factually correct. When information is incomplete or a question is difficult, a model may still generate a plausible answer instead of clearly identifying what it does not know.</p>
      <p>For example, an AI chatbot might provide the title of a scientific paper that does not exist or include an incorrect statistic in a polished, professional explanation. The answer may appear trustworthy because it is written fluently, but its presentation alone cannot establish its accuracy.</p>
      <p>This creates a potential problem for users. People often associate clear explanations and confident language with knowledge and expertise. However, an AI-generated answer can sound authoritative without being supported by reliable evidence.</p>
      <p>The important question, therefore, is not simply whether AI can make mistakes, but why those mistakes can be difficult to recognise.</p>

      <h2>4. Can We Detect When AI Is Wrong?</h2>
      <p>There is no single method that reliably identifies every incorrect AI answer. However, several approaches can help us evaluate a model's reliability.</p>
      <p><strong>False specificity.</strong> AI systems may generate plausible but nonexistent academic references, quotations, statistics, or other details. Important factual claims should be compared with reliable sources, such as peer-reviewed research, official documentation, or primary data. If a model provides references, those sources should be checked rather than accepted automatically.</p>
      <p><strong>Checking the reasoning.</strong> A correct final answer does not necessarily mean that the explanation leading to it is valid. In mathematics, science, and programming, examining the steps can reveal errors that the final result alone might hide.</p>
      <p>These methods can reduce the risk of accepting incorrect information, but none guarantees that every error will be detected. Understanding why AI produces convincing mistakes—and how its reliability can be measured—remains an important challenge in computer science.</p>

      <h2>5. Is AI Always to Blame for Its Mistakes?</h2>
      <p>When an AI system produces an incorrect answer, it is tempting to blame the technology immediately. However, the outcome can also depend on how the user interacts with the system. A vague, incomplete, or misleading prompt can make it more difficult for a model to understand what information is required.</p>
      <p>For example, asking an AI chatbot to "explain programming" leaves the subject, level of detail, and intended audience unclear. In contrast, asking it to "explain Python loops to a beginner and provide two simple examples" gives the model a clearer direction. A more specific prompt can improve the relevance and usefulness of a response, although it cannot guarantee factual accuracy.</p>
      <p>Users can also contribute to mistakes by providing false information in their prompts, failing to verify important claims, or treating an AI-generated response as unquestionably correct. If someone accepts an invented reference without checking it, the problem involves not only the system's output but also the user's decision to trust it without sufficient evidence.</p>
      <p>However, responsibility should not be shifted entirely onto users. AI systems can generate false claims even when questions are clear and well-written. A well-designed prompt cannot eliminate every limitation of a model.</p>
      <p>Therefore, understanding AI reliability requires examining both the limitations of the technology and the way people use it. Better prompts and responsible verification can help, but neither makes an AI system infallible.</p>

      <h2>6. Does Internet Access Affect AI's Accuracy?</h2>
      <p>Internet access can influence the accuracy and relevance of an AI system's answers, particularly when questions involve recent events, changing statistics, or information that may not have been available during training.</p>
      <p>A model that answers using only its learned parameters may lack the latest information. By contrast, a system equipped with web-search or retrieval tools may be able to consult current sources before generating a response. This can help it provide more up-to-date answers, provided that the sources are reliable and interpreted correctly.</p>
      <p>Nevertheless, having internet access does not automatically make an AI system accurate. Search results can contain outdated, misleading, or incorrect information, and a model may misunderstand or misrepresent what a source says. Similarly, an AI system without live internet access can still answer many questions correctly using what it learned during training.</p>
      <p>It is also important to distinguish internet access from internet speed. A slow connection may delay a response or interrupt a search, but a fast connection does not necessarily improve the factual accuracy of an answer. The more important factors are whether the system can access relevant information, the quality of that information, and how effectively it uses the evidence.</p>
      <p>Ultimately, AI reliability depends on several interacting factors: the model itself, the information available to it, the prompt provided by the user, and the way its output is evaluated. Neither the technology nor the user should be considered the sole cause of every mistake.</p>

      <h2>7. Why AI Can Struggle with Mathematics More Than Other Subjects</h2>
      <p>I wanted to test AI with mathematical problems that required more than basic calculations. I gave ChatGPT an infinite exponent tower problem, asking it to find a real number whose repeated powers approach two. ChatGPT concluded that the answer was the square root of two, which was correct.</p>
      <p>I also tested Gemini with a functional equation, asking it to find all continuous functions satisfying a particular mathematical relationship. Gemini gave cosine functions, hyperbolic cosine functions, and the zero function as solutions, which is also correct.</p>
      <p>However, when I tested both AI systems with simpler mathematical questions, surprisingly, both of them failed. This raises an interesting question: why can AI sometimes handle complex mathematical problems but make mistakes in basic arithmetic?</p>
      <p>One reason is that AI generates answers differently from a calculator. It learns patterns from large amounts of data and uses them to produce responses, rather than automatically following a guaranteed arithmetic procedure every time. As a result, it can make simple mistakes, such as miscalculating a number or losing track of a step in a longer calculation.</p>
      <p>This does not mean AI cannot solve mathematical problems. Rather, mathematical reasoning and reliable calculation are not exactly the same ability. AI may explain an advanced concept well but still make an elementary arithmetic error.</p>

      <h2>Conclusion</h2>
      <p>AI has become a useful tool for learning, writing, programming, and solving problems. However, its ability to produce fluent explanations should not be confused with a guarantee of accuracy. AI systems can provide correct answers to difficult questions and still make mistakes on simpler ones. They can also produce explanations that appear convincing without fully supporting their conclusions.</p>
      <p>The best approach is neither to trust every AI-generated answer nor to dismiss AI as unreliable. Instead, we should evaluate its performance according to the task, verify important claims, and examine the reasoning behind its conclusions.</p>
      <p>Ultimately, the important question is not whether AI can answer every question perfectly, but how reliably it performs different tasks—and how we can recognise situations in which its answers need closer examination.</p>
